          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "Archaeologist Linda Manzanilla, of the Universidad Nacional Autónoma de México, has spent decades reconstructing daily life in Teotihuacan, the largest pre-Columbian city in the Americas. Rather than focus on the city's monumental pyramids, Manzanilla has concentrated on its dense residential neighborhoods, where ordinary residents lived in stone-walled apartment compounds. Her work combines careful excavation of these compounds with isotopic analysis of skeletal remains and a fine-grained spatial study of household activities, an approach often grouped under household archaeology. By comparing the chemical signatures of teeth and bones with the regional geology of central Mexico, Manzanilla and her collaborators have shown that the people buried within a single compound often grew up in different parts of Mesoamerica before settling at Teotihuacan. In her 2015 synthesis 'Cooperation and tensions in multiethnic corporate societies,' Manzanilla argues that this combined evidence portrays Teotihuacan as a multiethnic urban society whose neighborhoods drew residents from distant regional origins.",
