          "explanation": "The passage establishes two premises: (1) Kelsey withheld approval of thalidomide because the manufacturer's submission described effects in adult patients but supplied little direct evidence about effects on a developing fetus, and (2) Congress responded by passing the Kefauver-Harris Amendments, which for the first time required manufacturers to supply rigorous safety data before approval. The minimum inference these two premises jointly force is that the pre-1962 framework had been accepting adult-only evidence as adequate stand-in evidence for fetal safety — otherwise Kelsey would not have had to refuse approval on those grounds, and Congress would not have had to amend the framework to fix the gap. Choice B states exactly that hedged claim, using 'as though' to capture the framework's implicit substitution without overreaching.\n\nThe inference combines both premises. Premise (1) alone, about one manufacturer's submission, does not by itself tell us how the broader framework treated such evidence. Premise (2) alone, about a tightening of requirements, does not by itself specify what kind of evidence the old framework was substituting for what. Only the joint claim that the old framework treated adult-safety evidence as sufficient for fetal-safety conclusions is licensed.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A (Scope shift): Pushes the modest premise that the manufacturer's evidence was inadequate into an absolute claim that 'no premarket testing' was required at all. The passage says the manufacturer did submit studies — just studies that focused on adults — so the pre-1962 framework clearly required some testing. Choice A overreaches by collapsing 'inadequate evidence' into 'no evidence.'\n- Choice C (Surface match): Sounds consistent with the passage's historical framing — the framework predates the thalidomide episode, so it is tempting to conclude that its drafters simply did not know about prenatal risks. But the passage tells us only what the framework required as evidence, not what individual regulators recognized or understood. Whether regulators were aware of prenatal risks is a separate question from whether the framework required evidence about them, so the premises do not license this conclusion.\n- Choice D (Inverse): Directly contradicts premise (2). The passage states that the Kefauver-Harris Amendments required rigorous safety data 'for the first time,' which means the post-1962 system imposed stricter, not laxer, requirements than the framework it replaced."
