          "explanation": "The passage establishes two premises: (1) pointing accuracy for large spacecraft instruments is set at very fine angular tolerances, so even subtle on-board disturbances can knock the line of sight off target, and (2) on-board moving parts (reaction wheels, articulating mechanisms) couple with the flexible bending and twisting of the spacecraft, not with a perfectly rigid body. The minimum inference these two premises jointly force is that a prediction method which ignores either fact — that is, one that treats the spacecraft as rigid — is unlikely to be trustworthy, because the missing flexible-body coupling can produce angular errors that the tight pointing budget cannot absorb. Choice A states exactly that combined claim and stays within the evidence the passage actually provides.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B (Scope shift): Pushes a general statement about coupled disturbances into an absolute claim about 'a single articulating mechanism' being 'always' the dominant error source. The passage lists reaction wheels and articulating mechanisms as examples of moving parts that couple with the structure, but it does not single out one device or rank error sources, so the inference is too narrow and too strong.\n- Choice C (Surface match): Sounds consistent with rigorous engineering practice — comparing models is reasonable — but it directly contradicts premise (2). If moving parts couple with the flexible structure rather than a rigid body, then a rigid-body model and a flexible-body model of the same spacecraft should not be expected to converge at fine angular scales; the passage's premises are precisely what licenses the opposite expectation.\n- Choice D (Inverse): Reverses both premises at once. It claims that a reaction-wheel-only analysis is sufficient and that structural bending and twisting cannot matter at fine angular tolerances, which contradicts premise (2) (moving parts couple with the flexible structure) and premise (1) (fine tolerances make subtle disturbances consequential)."
