          "explanation": "The temporal anchor 'In 1957' fixes the action at a specific point in the past, and the surrounding sentences use simple past throughout ('designed,' 'showed,' 'was'). A completed historical event tied to a specific past date requires the simple past tense, so 'observed' is the correct verb form.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- (A) 'has observed' is present perfect, which presents an action as ongoing or still-relevant up to the present. It is incompatible with the fixed past date 'In 1957,' which closes the time frame. This is the surface-match distractor: present perfect resembles the correct historical-relevance framing but conflicts with the explicit past-date anchor.\n- (B) 'had observed' is past perfect, which is used to mark an action completed before another past event. There is no earlier reference event in the passage to which Wu's observation must be prior, so past perfect introduces a sequence that does not exist. This is the scope-shift distractor: a different verb-tense error from the one being tested.\n- (D) 'observes' is simple present, the inverse of the correct simple past. It clashes directly with 'In 1957' and with the past-tense narration of the surrounding sentences."
