          "correctAnswer": "B",
          "explanation": "The correct answer is B, \"Rome;\". The verb \"to test\" governs a three-item series of direct objects: (1) \"long-standing claims about migration into Imperial Rome,\" (2) \"reconstructions of ancient diets, especially among the urban poor,\" and (3) \"assessments of mortality patterns in working-class cemeteries outside the city walls.\" Because item (2) contains its own internal comma (\"diets, especially among the urban poor\"), an ordinary comma between items would blur where one item ends and the next begins. Standard English handles this by promoting the list separator from a comma to a semicolon — the so-called \"super-comma\" — so the reader can see the item boundaries clearly. The passage already uses this convention between items (2) and (3) (\"…urban poor; and assessments…\"); the same separator is required between items (1) and (2). Choice B places a semicolon at that boundary, matching the higher-order separator the rest of the series uses.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice A (\"Rome,\"): surface match — a comma is the default separator for items in a series, and it would be correct here if none of the items contained internal commas. But because item (2) is internally comma-marked, a comma at this slot is indistinguishable from item (2)'s own internal comma, so the reader cannot tell whether \"reconstructions of ancient diets\" begins a new list item or continues the first one. The presence of the semicolon already in use later in the series (\"; and assessments…\") signals that this is a semicolon-series, not a comma-series.\n- Choice C (\"Rome:\"): scope shift — a different SEC error. A colon is used to introduce an elaboration, list, or explanation that follows a complete grammatical thought, not to separate items inside an already-running series. Placing a colon between items (1) and (2) would mistakenly recast item (2) as a definition or explanation of item (1), which the surrounding syntax (and the existing \"; and\" separator before item 3) does not support.\n- Choice D (\"Rome\"): inverse — the opposite of the correct treatment. Omitting boundary punctuation entirely fuses item (1) into item (2): \"…claims about migration into Imperial Rome reconstructions of ancient diets…\" runs the noun phrase \"Imperial Rome\" directly into the next noun phrase \"reconstructions of ancient diets\" with no mark at all, producing an ungrammatical run-together. Some matched separator — and given item (2)'s internal comma, specifically a semicolon — is required."
