          "type": "multiple-choice",
          "difficulty": "hard",
          "band": 4,
          "domain": "expression-of-ideas",
          "skill": "transitions",
          "passage": "Theoretical ecologists increasingly argue that interannual climate variability can leave detectable fingerprints on the timing of recurrent infectious-disease epidemics, provided that long, dense case records and mechanistically grounded statistical models are available to separate climate forcing from the internal cycles of population immunity. ______ Mercedes Pascual's analyses of decades of Bangladeshi cholera records show that outbreak peaks there track the El Niño–Southern Oscillation closely enough for the climate signal to be statistically distinguished from the immune-driven oscillations her models recover in the very same data.",
          "question": "Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?",
          "choices": [
            {
              "id": "A",
              "text": "Likewise,"
            },
            {
              "id": "B",
              "text": "Moreover,"
            },
            {
              "id": "C",
              "text": "For instance,"
            },
            {
              "id": "D",
              "text": "By contrast,"
            }
          ],
          "correctAnswer": "C",
          "explanation": "The first sentence makes a general methodological claim about the field: theoretical ecologists argue that interannual climate variability can leave detectable fingerprints on the timing of recurrent infectious-disease epidemics, so long as long case records and mechanistically grounded statistical models are available to separate climate forcing from the internal immunity-driven cycles. The second sentence then names a specific empirical case — Pascual's analyses of Bangladeshi cholera and the El Niño–Southern Oscillation — that exemplifies the general claim by satisfying its conditions: a long case-record series, a mechanistic model, and a successfully separated climate signal. The relation between the two sentences is general principle to specific illustrating case. 'For instance,' is the CB-lexicon transition for that general-to-specific move and is correct (C).\n\nThe cue that selects 'For instance,' here is the shift in specificity: sentence 1 talks abstractly about 'recurrent infectious-disease epidemics' and the conditions under which climate fingerprints are detectable; sentence 2 names a particular country (Bangladesh), a particular disease (cholera), and a particular climate index (the El Niño–Southern Oscillation) that together form one concrete realization of the abstract claim — the textbook signature of an illustrating instance.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- (A) 'Likewise,' (surface match — different but plausible logical relation) signals continuation by parallel: it would fit if sentence 1 had already named a specific case and sentence 2 named a second, parallel one (e.g., 'Influenza in temperate cities tracks winter humidity. Likewise, cholera in Bangladesh tracks ENSO.'). Sentence 1 here, however, names no prior specific case — it states a general principle — so there is nothing for sentence 2 to be parallel *to*. 'Likewise,' is the strongest distractor because Pascual's finding does feel continuous with sentence 1, but the relation is illustrating a general claim, not paralleling a prior specific one.\n- (B) 'Moreover,' (scope shift — too weak for the relation) signals that sentence 2 contributes one more point on the same line of thought as sentence 1. That is too flat: sentence 2 is not an additional general claim placed alongside the methodological argument of sentence 1; it is a specific empirical case offered as evidence for that general claim. 'Moreover,' would fit if sentence 2 introduced a separate, parallel principle (for example, that immunity cycles also need long records) rather than a single illustrating study.\n- (D) 'By contrast,' (inverse — opposite logical move) signals that sentence 2 cuts against sentence 1, naming a case in which the general claim fails. The Pascual finding does the opposite: it satisfies the conditions sentence 1 lays out (long case records, mechanistic models) and recovers the predicted climate signature, confirming the general claim with a concrete case rather than complicating it. 'By contrast,' points in exactly the wrong direction."
