          "explanation": "The correct answer is A, \"a function of.\" The passage establishes that Hartman works almost entirely from records produced by police, social workers, and reformers — institutions whose purpose was to surveil and discipline the very women she means to recover. The sentence with the blank then says that what Hartman is able to recover (intimacies and forms of freedom) stands in some specific relation to her methodological inversion, the practice of reading those coercive records against their original purposes. The blank therefore needs a phrase that names a relation of dependence: the recovery is produced by, and would not exist without, the inversion. \"A function of\" carries exactly that relation in academic register — to say one thing is a function of another is to say the first is determined by, and arises out of, the second. Hartman's recovery is enabled by the methodological move; the inversion is its generative condition.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B: surface match — \"in keeping with\" collocates fluently with \"methodology\" and \"approach\" in scholarly writing, so the phrase feels at home in a sentence about a historian's practice. But \"in keeping with\" means consistent with or in harmony with: it names a relation of agreement between two things that already exist independently. The sentence is not claiming that Hartman's recovery merely matches her methodology in tone or spirit; it is claiming that the recovery is brought into being by the methodological inversion. The phrase has the right register without the right logical relation.\n- Choice C: scope shift — \"of a piece with\" lives in the same general neighborhood as \"in keeping with\" and is sometimes reached for to name a tight relation between elements of a project. Its scope, however, is taxonomic rather than causal: to be of a piece with something is to be of the same kind as it, part of the same continuous fabric. The sentence requires a relation in which one term produces the other, not a relation in which both belong to the same category. The phrase shifts the relation from cause-and-effect to mere kinship.\n- Choice D: inverse — \"at odds with\" is the near-opposite of what the sentence requires. The whole point of the paragraph is that the recovery and the methodological inversion are continuous: the inversion is the means by which the recovery becomes possible. To say the recovery is at odds with the inversion would be to say the two are in conflict, reversing the dependency the passage spells out and stranding Hartman's project without an account of how she arrives at her findings."
