          "passage": "The following text is adapted from Henry James's 1898 novella \"The Turn of the Screw.\" On Christmas Eve, a small company has gathered around the fire at an old country house, where one of their number, Douglas, has spoken of a private document in his keeping.\n\nThe story we had just been told, round our hearthstone, was sufficiently dreadful — a child waked in the night by a visitation no nurse could account for — and the silence that followed it was the silence of a company already fortified, in advance, against any further turn of horror. It was Douglas, however, who broke that silence, not to add to what we had heard, but to gesture, with the diffidence of a man unwilling to seem theatrical, toward something held in reserve. There was a record, he allowed at last, in a woman's hand, which had come into his possession years before; if we were patient, and would consent to wait the three days required for its retrieval, he would consent, in turn, to read it aloud. The fire burned a little lower; our chairs drew nearer; and the thing, though still absent from the room, began already to lay its claim upon us.",
