          "passage": "Bioarchaeologist Yilmaz Selim Erdal, of Hacettepe University in Ankara, studies skeletal remains recovered from past communities of Anatolia in order to ask how shifts in subsistence shaped the bodies of those who lived through them. In a 2012 study of the Iron Age village of Hakemi Use in southeastern Turkey, Erdal examined patterns of joint disease and dental wear across the burials excavated at the site. He found that degenerative changes in the spine, knees, and shoulders were unusually common and that the teeth showed heavy occlusal wear consistent with a diet built around coarsely ground cereals. Rather than treat these features as ordinary signs of aging, Erdal read them together as evidence that life at Hakemi Use was organized around sustained, physically demanding agricultural labor and a grain-heavy diet. By interpreting the skeletal patterns as a coordinated record of how villagers worked and ate, the study makes the case that bioarchaeological evidence can reconstruct the lived rhythm of an Iron Age farming community.",
