          "skill": "central-ideas-and-details",
          "passage": "In his 1973 essay \"Encoding/Decoding,\" cultural theorist Stuart Hall challenged the then-common view that television communicated meaning straightforwardly from broadcaster to viewer. Hall argued that producers encode programs within particular ideological frames, selecting images, language, and narrative conventions that quietly carry a preferred reading of events. Audiences, he observed, do not simply absorb that reading. Instead, they decode the message from their own social positions and may take up a dominant interpretation that aligns with the producers' frame, a negotiated interpretation that accepts parts while resisting others, or an oppositional interpretation that rejects the frame outright. For Hall, who developed the model while working in British cultural studies, this gap between encoded intent and decoded reception meant that television could no longer be treated as a neutral transmitter; it was a site where ideological meaning was produced on one side and actively reworked on the other.",
