          "passage": "In her 1980 essay \"Compulsory Heterosexuality and Lesbian Existence,\" the poet and critic Adrienne Rich is often described as having broadened feminist criticism to take lesbian experience seriously as a subject of literary and social analysis. That description, scholars have noted, captures only part of what the essay sets out to do. Rich's central move is not to add lesbian experience to an existing feminist agenda but to reframe the surrounding category itself: she argues that heterosexuality should be examined as a political institution sustained by social and economic pressures, rather than treated as a natural default against which other arrangements are measured. From that reframing, Rich proposes a continuum of woman-identified experience that she sees running underneath those pressures. The essay's significance, on this reading, lies less in its widening of feminist criticism's topics than in its insistence that the institution most often left unexamined be brought into view and analyzed on the same terms as the arrangements it has historically defined as exceptions.",
