          "correctAnswer": "A",
          "explanation": "The passage establishes two premises. First, urban schools serving the poorest neighborhoods have been asked to compensate for inequities — housing instability, joblessness, unequal health care — that originate outside the school and lie beyond educators' direct control. Second, reform packages aimed only at what happens inside the schoolhouse (new curricula, accountability systems, school leaders) have produced short-term gains that fade once the surrounding neighborhood conditions reassert themselves. The minimum inference these two premises jointly force is that durable improvement is unlikely unless the reform reaches the surrounding conditions the schools have been asked to compensate for. Choice A captures exactly that paired claim, linking instructional reform to neighborhood-level economic and housing policy without overreaching.\n\nThe stem requires the conclusion that the two premises together license. Premise (1) alone tells us only that schools face external inequities; it does not by itself prescribe a remedy. Premise (2) alone tells us that inside-the-schoolhouse reform fades; it does not by itself say what would not fade. Only the joint claim — that improvement requires reform paired with neighborhood-level policy — is licensed.\n\nWhy the wrong answers are tempting:\n- Choice B (Inverse): Directly contradicts premise (2). The passage states that reform concentrated on curriculum, accountability, and school leadership has repeatedly produced gains that fade once neighborhood conditions reassert themselves, so doubling down on those same levers is precisely the strategy the premises identify as insufficient.\n- Choice C (Scope shift): Pushes a measured claim about pairing reform with neighborhood policy into an absolute requirement that 'every urban district' adopt 'an identical package' 'simultaneously.' The premises concern what kind of reform is durable, not how uniformly or concurrently it must be implemented across districts, so the universal-and-identical claim outruns the evidence.\n- Choice D (Surface match): Sounds consistent with the passage's emphasis on neighborhood conditions, but it overshoots in the opposite direction by demanding that reformers 'abandon any focus on curriculum and accountability.' The premises identify inside-the-schoolhouse reform as insufficient on its own, not as something to be discarded; the licensed inference is pairing, not replacement."
