          "domain": "information-and-ideas",
          "skill": "inferences",
          "passage": "In her 2022 book South to America, Imani Perry of Harvard challenges a habit common in American public discourse: treating the South as a regional curiosity, exceptional rather than representative. Drawing on travel narrative, archival research, and cultural criticism, Perry traces how arrangements first worked out in the South—concerning labor, citizenship, racial hierarchy, and the structure of the economy—were not contained there but were absorbed into national institutions and continue to organize life across the country. She also argues that political and economic conflicts that observers tend to frame as national in origin become legible only when readers attend to their distinctly Southern roots. Perry's account therefore implies that any analysis of the United States that treats the South as peripheral ______",
